 First, we note that by the AM-GM inequality, we have
\begin{align*}
\frac{a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2}{4} &\ge \sqrt[4]{a^2b^2c^2d^2}, \\
\frac{a^3 + b^3 + c^3 + d^3}{4} &\ge \sqrt[4]{a^3b^3c^3d^3}.
\end{align*}
Multiplying these two inequalities, we get
\begin{align*}
\frac{(a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2)(a^3 + b^3 + c^3 + d^3)}{16} &\ge \sqrt[4]{a^2b^2c^2d^2} \cdot \sqrt[4]{a^3b^3c^3d^3} \\
&= abcd.
\end{align*}
Since $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 = 4$, we have
\begin{align*}
a^3 + b^3 + c^3 + d^3 &\ge 4 \sqrt[4]{abcd} \\
&= 4 \sqrt[4]{a^2b^2c^2d^2} \\
&= 4^{3/4} \cdot \sqrt[4]{(a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2)^2} \\
&= 4^{3/4} \cdot 2 \\
&= 8 \sqrt[4]{2}.
\end{align*}
Therefore, we have
\begin{align*}
(a + b + c + d)(a^3 + b^3 + c^3 + d^3) &\ge (a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2)^2 \\
&= 4^2 \\
&= 16.
\end{align*}
Equality holds when $a = b = c = d = 1$, so the only possible solution is $\boxed{(a,b,c,d) = (1,1,1,1)}$. </think> <answer> 1 </answer>
